{"file_path":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/defect_cocycle_with_findings.tex","new_string":"The object relocates the difficulty rather than removing it. The experiments show\n$\\Dcoll$ behaves as advertised on the parts that are reorganizations of known\nstructure---the congruence, the annealed gap, the Cram\\'er tilt, the post-exit\ntaboo, and a genuine finite operator gap weakest at the critical tilt---while the\none open arrow (a persistent quenched bias forcing a top finite-level eigenstate)\nremains open, and the single-orbit shadow that would witness it is confounded by\nfinite size exactly as the circularity leak predicts.\n\n\\appendix\n\\section{The quadratic-character coboundary: an analytic proof}\n\\label{app:quad}\n\nThis appendix proves the structural fact found numerically: for the faithful\nresidue--height skew product, the quadratic character $\\chi_2$ of $(\\Z/3^r)^\\times$\nis an exact coboundary, so $\\rho(\\Lcal_{\\chi_2})=\\rho(\\Lcal_{\\mathbf 1})$ and its\ngap vanishes; whereas every character of order $>2$ stays gapped. Write\n$b_i=v_2(3x_i+1)$, $B_n=\\sum_{i<n}b_i$, and $S(x)=(3x+1)/2^{b}$.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[$\\chi_2$ factors through reduction mod $3$]\\label{lem:fac3}\nFor $r\\ge1$, $(\\Z/3^r)^\\times$ is cyclic of order $\\phi=2\\cdot3^{r-1}$ and the\nquadratic character $\\chi_2$ (its unique order-$2$ character) satisfies\n$\\ker\\chi_2=\\{u:\\,u\\equiv1\\!\\!\\pmod 3\\}$. Equivalently $\\chi_2(a)=\\left(\\tfrac a3\\right)$,\nthe Legendre symbol mod $3$: $\\chi_2(a)=+1$ if $a\\equiv1$ and $-1$ if $a\\equiv2\\pmod3$.\n\\end{lemma}\n\\begin{proof}\n$2$ is a primitive root mod $3^r$, so the group is cyclic of order $\\phi$ and\n$\\ker\\chi_2$ is its unique index-$2$ subgroup, the squares, of order $3^{r-1}$.\nReduction $(\\Z/3^r)^\\times\\!\\to(\\Z/3)^\\times\\cong\\Z/2$ has kernel\n$\\{u\\equiv1\\!\\pmod3\\}$, also of order $3^{r-1}$. A cyclic group has a unique\nsubgroup of each order, so the two coincide; hence $\\chi_2$ depends only on\n$a\\bmod3$, equalling $+1$ on $a\\equiv1$ and $-1$ on $a\\equiv2$.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{proposition}[Key identity]\\label{prop:key}\nFor every odd $x$, with $b=v_2(3x+1)$,\n\\[\n\\chi_2\\!\\left(S(x)\\bmod 3^r\\right)=(-1)^{b}.\n\\]\nIn particular the character of the image depends only on $b$, not on $x$, and\n$\\chi_2(x_{i+1})=(-1)^{b_i}$ along any orbit.\n\\end{proposition}\n\\begin{proof}\nWork mod $3$. Since $2\\equiv-1\\pmod3$ and $3x+1\\equiv1\\pmod3$,\n\\[\nS(x)=(3x+1)\\,2^{-b}\\equiv 1\\cdot(-1)^{-b}\\equiv(-1)^{b}\\pmod 3 .\n\\]\nThus $S(x)\\equiv1\\pmod3$ iff $b$ is even and $S(x)\\equiv2\\pmod3$ iff $b$ is odd;\nby \\cref{lem:fac3}, $\\chi_2(S(x))=(-1)^b$. (Note $3x+1\\equiv1\\pmod3$ makes $S(x)$ a\nunit, so $\\chi_2(S(x))\\ne0$.)\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{corollary}[The running character is the valuation parity]\\label{cor:run}\n$\\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^{n}\\chi_2(x_i)=(-1)^{B_n}$, and\n$\\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}\\chi_2(x_i)=\\chi_2(x_0)(-1)^{B_{n-1}}$. The quadratic character\naccumulated along the orbit is exactly $(-1)$ to the cumulative $2$-adic valuation.\n\\end{corollary}\n\\begin{proof}\nImmediate from \\cref{prop:key}: $\\prod_{i=1}^n\\chi_2(x_i)=\\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}(-1)^{b_i}=(-1)^{B_n}$.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{theorem}[$\\chi_2$ is a coboundary of the residue--valuation skew product]\n\\label{thm:cobound}\nOn the skew product with state $(a,\\tau)\\in(\\Z/3^r)^\\times\\times(\\Z/2)$ and\ndynamics $(a,\\tau)\\xrightarrow{b}\\big((3a+1)2^{-b},\\,\\tau+b\\big)$, set\n$d(a,\\tau)=\\chi_2(a)\\,(-1)^{\\tau}\\in\\{\\pm1\\}$. Then on every edge\n\\[\n\\frac{d(a',\\tau')}{d(a,\\tau)}=\\chi_2(a).\n\\]\nConsequently the twisted annealed transfer operator obeys\n$\\Lcal_{\\chi_2}=D\\,\\Lcal_{\\mathbf 1}\\,D^{-1}$ with $D=\\mathrm{diag}(d)$, so\n$\\rho(\\Lcal_{\\chi_2})=\\rho(\\Lcal_{\\mathbf 1})$ and $\\kappa=0$, for every tilt $s$\nand any height discretization.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nBy \\cref{prop:key}, $\\chi_2(a')=(-1)^b$, and $\\tau'=\\tau+b$ gives\n$(-1)^{\\tau'}=(-1)^\\tau(-1)^b=(-1)^\\tau\\chi_2(a')$. Hence, using $\\chi_2(a')^2=1$,\n\\[\nd(a',\\tau')=\\chi_2(a')(-1)^{\\tau'}=\\chi_2(a')\\,(-1)^\\tau\\chi_2(a')=(-1)^\\tau,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{d(a',\\tau')}{d(a,\\tau)}=\\frac{(-1)^\\tau}{\\chi_2(a)(-1)^\\tau}=\\chi_2(a).\n\\]\nWith the character carried on the source, the matrix element of $D^{-1}\\Lcal_{\\chi_2}D$\non this edge is $d(a',\\tau')^{-1}\\,w_s(b)\\chi_2(a)\\,d(a,\\tau)=w_s(b)$, the matrix\nelement of $\\Lcal_{\\mathbf 1}$. So the operators are conjugate by the real\northogonal $D$, hence isospectral.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\paragraph{Why the residue alone is gapped, and the exact ratio.} Without the\n$\\tau$-coordinate, $\\chi_2$ is \\emph{not} a coboundary: the candidate potential\nwould be $a$-only, forcing $(-1)^b=1$. Indeed $\\chi_2$ factors through mod $3$, on\nwhich the dynamics is $\\epsilon\\mapsto(-1)^b$ on $\\{\\pm1\\}$; the $2\\times2$ twisted\noperator $M_{\\chi_2}[\\epsilon',\\epsilon]=\\epsilon\\sum_{b:(-1)^b=\\epsilon'}w_s(b)$ has\neigenvalues $0$ and $\\sum_b(-1)^b w_s(b)$, giving the closed form\n\\[\n\\frac{\\rho(\\Lcal^{\\mathrm{res}}_{\\chi_2})}{\\rho(\\Lcal^{\\mathrm{res}}_{\\mathbf 1})}\n=\\frac{\\bigl|\\sum_b(-1)^b w_s(b)\\bigr|}{\\sum_b w_s(b)}\n=\\frac{2^{1+s}-1}{2^{1+s}+1}\n\\quad\\Big(=\\tfrac13\\text{ at }s{=}0,\\ \\tfrac35\\text{ at }s{=}1\\Big),\n\\]\nmatching Experiment F exactly. Adding $\\tau$ supplies the coordinate that absorbs\nthe alternating sign and lifts the ratio to $1$.\n\n\\begin{remark}[Only the quadratic character]\nFor a character $\\chi$ of order $d>2$, $\\chi(3a+1)$ is \\emph{not} constant: by\n\\cref{lem:fac3} $3a+1$ is a square, and $\\chi$ restricted to the squares (order\n$3^{r-1}$) is nontrivial when $d>2$. Then $\\chi(a')=\\chi(3a+1)\\chi(2)^{-b}$ depends\non $a$, the per-edge phase is not a function of $(b,\\tau)$ alone, and no diagonal\n$D$ on the skew product trivialises it---so $\\chi$ stays gapped. This is exactly\nwhy the maximum gap ratio over the full group equals $1$ only at $k=\\phi/2$.\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\begin{remark}[Height connection; \\texttt{sync} vs \\texttt{factored}]\nThe valuation parity is the height's parity: $H_n=n\\log_2 3-B_n+O(1)$, so\n$\\tau=B_n\\bmod2$ is carried by the residue--height state in the \\texttt{sync}\noperator (one $b$ drives residue and height together), which therefore inherits\nthe coboundary and shows $\\kappa_Q=0$ for all $R,L$---as observed. The\n\\texttt{factored} operator draws the height's valuation \\emph{independently} of the\nresidue's $b$, so $\\tau$ no longer records the residue valuation, the coboundary\nis broken, and the gap returns to the residue value\n$1-\\tfrac{2^{1+s}-1}{2^{1+s}+1}$ (and the worst-character $\\kappa_Q$ stabilises).\nAll claims here were checked numerically: the identity of \\cref{prop:key} over\n$10^5$ seeds, \\cref{cor:run} along every tested orbit, and\n\\cref{thm:cobound} by direct diagonalisation (residue-only ratio $1/3$;\nresidue$\\times$parity ratio $1$).\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\end{document}","old_string":"The object relocates the difficulty rather than removing it. The experiments show\n$\\Dcoll$ behaves as advertised on the parts that are reorganizations of known\nstructure---the congruence, the annealed gap, the Cram\\'er tilt, the post-exit\ntaboo, and a genuine finite operator gap weakest at the critical tilt---while the\none open arrow (a persistent quenched bias forcing a top finite-level eigenstate)\nremains open, and the single-orbit shadow that would witness it is confounded by\nfinite size exactly as the circularity leak predicts.\n\n\\end{document}","replace_all":false}